From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:17:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 152/153] test(gascity): claim-command tests assert sleep
 counts, not a 2s wall clock (gcp-tzai, gcp-dy0qj)

Two claim-command tests ran gascity/commands/claim/run.sh under
subprocess.run(timeout=2) and flaked under host load (gcp-tzai; the base-red
bead gcp-dy0qj tracks the same flake). run.sh spawns one python3 per
json_pick field, 12 to 16 spawns per run, which measured 0.9 to 1.3s at load
14 and blew the 2s budget at load 95+.

The 2s budget was standing in for an assertion that the PATH sleep shim
replaced the real sleep (run.sh sleeps 2s between hook attempts and 1s between
verify attempts). The retry count is already asserted by call count. So:

- one module constant, CLAIM_COMMAND_HANG_GUARD_SECONDS = 30, replaces the
  three timeout=2 sites (the termination-signal test changes timeout only);
- in the bounds and unreadable-claim tests the fake sleep now logs
  "sleep <arg>" to its own GC_TEST_SLEEPS file and returns without sleeping.
  A separate file keeps the existing GC_TEST_CALLS line-count assertions
  unchanged;
- new assertions pin the sleeps: ["sleep 2", "sleep 2"] for the hook loop and
  ["sleep 1", "sleep 1"] for the verify loop, i.e. the shim ran and the loop
  slept exactly between its 3 attempts.

Validation: mutating run.sh:184 sleep 2 -> 3 fails the bounds test on the
new sleep assertion, and run.sh:241 sleep 1 -> 3 fails the unreadable test
(both reverted). Out of scope: run.sh's per-field python3 spawns, an upstream
performance change rather than a test fix.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
